"""Finite-rule construction and positive-characteristic cancellation."""

def append_algebra(p, eq, h, sub, page):
    page('16. A finite hierarchy of word rules')
    p('A word presentation consists of replacements <i>u</i> ↔ <i>v</i> of equal positive length and declarations <i>w</i> = 0. A replacement may be used inside any word, in either direction. Over a field <i>K</i> the corresponding algebra is the free unital associative algebra modulo the two-sided ideal generated by <i>u</i> − <i>v</i> and the declared zero words.')
    p('<b>Lemma 16.1 (Word basis).</b> <i>The nonzero replacement classes form a basis over any field. A word is zero exactly when a finite sequence of replacements reaches a word containing a declared zero factor.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> Form the graph on all words with contextual replacements as edges. The span of edge differences is the span of all differences within a connected component. The quotient therefore has one basis vector per component. Multiplication concatenates components, so imposing the zero declarations kills exactly the components meeting one of their contexts. All rules preserve length, and none affects the empty word. □')
    p('Fix <i>c</i> = 10000, <i>k</i> = 10 and <i>D</i> = 2<sup>21</sup>. In particular <i>D</i> &gt; 10(<i>c</i> + 1)(<i>k</i> + 1). The construction below uses no field operations. For all sufficiently large integer parameters <i>h</i>, put <i>q</i> = 2<sup>h</sup> and <i>L</i><sub>h</sub> = 2<i>q</i> + 1.')
    p('<b>Theorem 16.2 (Hierarchy).</b> <i>There are an integer h<sub>0</sub> and finite word presentations A<sub>h</sub>(K), for h = h<sub>0</sub>D<sup>j</sup>, j ≥ 0, with the following properties, over every field K.</i>')
    p('<b>(a)</b> <i>The alphabet Σ<sub>h</sub> has size 2<sup>ch</sup> and every defining rule has length at most k. A substitution E<sub>h</sub> encodes an upper letter as a word of length L<sub>h</sub>, induces A<sub>Dh</sub> → A<sub>h</sub>, and preserves nonzero words. Each level has a nonzero letter.</i>')
    p('<b>(b)</b> <i>A subset of letters defines cuts immediately before their occurrences. Every sufficiently long nonzero word decomposes uniquely into a bounded prefix, the intervals between its consecutive cuts, and a bounded suffix. Each such interval t can be changed internally to E<sub>h</sub>(u<sub>t</sub>), where 1 ≤ |u<sub>t</sub>| ≤ k, so |t| = L<sub>h</sub>|u<sub>t</sub>|. The token list and the bounds are finite.</i>')
    p('<b>(c)</b> <i>There are q palette colors. Every sufficiently long nonzero word contains a run of at least q/100 palette letters with identical non-color fields and no control heads. Within every such run, adjacent letters may be transposed and all colors are distinct. Its eligibility and maximal endpoints depend only on the non-color fields.</i>')
    p('The following pages specify the finite membership predicate for the rule table and prove these assertions. Enumeration of its finite input set gives an ordinary finite presentation at any chosen level; relations from other levels are never added as extra relators.')

    page('16. Alphabet and structural blocks')
    p('Let Σ<sub>h</sub> be all bit strings of length <i>ch</i>. Reserve 32 integer fields of width 2<i>h</i> and 512 one-bit fields, using fixed disjoint slots. Name the fields as needed below, in the order of their introduction; fill all unused slots with zero. Integers use binary notation. Finite tags use the initial available flag slots. Every out-of-range value or unlisted combination is a unary zero. The listed fields occupy fewer than these fixed allowances. Large constants occurring in the computation program are encoded in data, not in the alphabet format.')
    p('A letter has one structural type: <i>C</i>(<i>r</i>) with 1 ≤ <i>r</i> ≤ <i>q</i>, a palette letter <i>P</i>(<i>a</i>) with 1 ≤ <i>a</i> ≤ <i>q</i>, or a stop <i>S</i>. The permitted neighboring types are precisely')
    eq('<i>C</i>(<i>r</i>)<i>C</i>(<i>r</i>+1), &nbsp; <i>C</i>(<i>q</i>)<i>P</i>(<i>a</i>), &nbsp; <i>P</i>(<i>a</i>)<i>P</i>(<i>b</i>),<br/><i>P</i>(<i>a</i>)<i>S</i>, &nbsp; <i>SC</i>(1).')
    p('Here the first type requires <i>r</i> &lt; <i>q</i>; all unlisted pairs are zero. A full structural block consists of <i>q</i> core cells, a nonempty palette, and one stop. No test is made for a neighbor absent at an endpoint of the whole word.')
    p('Each letter has a transaction track. Each palette letter also has a checker track; on other structural types the checker fields are idle and empty. An idle core cell has a data bit at positions <i>r</i> ≤ <i>cDh</i>, and no data bit at other positions. We will choose <i>h</i> large enough that <i>cDh</i> ≤ <i>q</i>. Palette and stop cells have no data bits. A canonical block has both tracks idle, each palette color once in increasing order, and its <i>cDh</i> core data bits equal to an upper letter in Σ<sub>Dh</sub>. This defines <i>E</i><sub>h</sub> on every upper bit string, even those later declared zero. Its length is <i>L</i><sub>h</sub> = 2<i>q</i> + 1.')
    p('A nonidle transaction cell has a marker <i>L</i>, <i>R</i>, <i>B</i>, or <i>F</i>: respectively a left wing, right wing, acquisition cursor, or verification head. It carries a proposed number of blocks 1 ≤ <i>l</i> ≤ <i>k</i>, a task tag “replacement” or “zero”, and a relative block number 1 ≤ <i>b</i> ≤ <i>l</i>. Only a cursor or full head carries the selected data side ε ∈ {0,1}; a zero task always has ε = 0. Only a full head carries the verifier control and three reading bits.')
    p('A nonidle core cell has two data slots at each position <i>r</i> ≤ <i>cDh</i>, and one certificate bit at every core position. Other cells have no payload. A nonidle cell is at its known first position when <i>b</i> = 1 and its type is <i>C</i>(1); it is at its known last position when <i>b</i> = <i>l</i> and its type is <i>S</i>. An <i>R</i> at a known first position and an <i>L</i> at a known last position are unary zeros. These tests apply even when a whole-word neighbor is absent.')

    page('16. The local interface rules')
    p('A side of a transaction cell has an internal or external interface. The complete list is as follows. “First” and “last” refer to the known positions just defined.')
    p('<b>Idle.</b> Both interfaces are external.')
    p('<b>Left wing L.</b> The left interface is external at first, otherwise internal from an <i>L</i>. The right interface is internal to <i>L</i>, <i>B</i>, or <i>F</i>.')
    p('<b>Cursor B.</b> The left interface is external at first, otherwise internal from an <i>L</i>. The right interface is external.')
    p('<b>Full head F.</b> The left interface is external at first, otherwise internal from an <i>L</i>. The right interface is external at last, otherwise internal to an <i>R</i>.')
    p('<b>Right wing R.</b> The left interface is internal from <i>F</i> or <i>R</i>. The right interface is external at last, otherwise internal to an <i>R</i>.')
    p('Facing interfaces must both be external, or both be internal with the prescribed markers. An internal pair has identical task and <i>l</i>; its relative block number is unchanged within a block and increases by one across <i>SC</i>(1). It cannot enter a known first position or leave a known last position. Every pair also satisfies the structural test. These are all transaction pair tests; any violation is a binary zero.')
    p('The checker on a palette has states idle, passed(<i>x</i>), or head(<i>x</i>), for a sought color 1 ≤ <i>x</i> ≤ <i>q</i>. Its permitted neighboring states are exactly')
    eq('(idle,idle), &nbsp; (passed(<i>x</i>),passed(<i>x</i>)),<br/>(passed(<i>x</i>),head(<i>x</i>)), &nbsp; (head(<i>x</i>),idle).')
    p('At the first palette cell any of these states is permitted. At its last cell only idle or head is permitted, tested by its pair with <i>S</i>. The tests at the preceding <i>C</i>(<i>q</i>) and at <i>S</i> are binary. Thus a complete palette is either idle or a passed prefix, one checker head, and an idle suffix. Partial versions are allowed at endpoints of the whole word.')
    p('A complete acquired region consequently has the form <i>L</i>*<i>F</i><i>R</i>* on exactly its <i>l</i> blocks. A partially acquired region is <i>L</i>*<i>B</i>, possibly ending inside a block, with the rest of that block idle. Adjacent regions may have different metadata at an external joint. These consequences follow from the pair tests; no assumption that a word arose by a computation is imposed.')

    page('16. Palette moves and acquisition')
    p('All replacements preserve every field not explicitly changed, and include their inverses. At <i>C</i>(<i>q</i>) followed by an idle-checker palette cell, a checker head seeking any <i>x</i> may be created. On neighboring palette cells, (head(<i>x</i>),idle) may become (passed(<i>x</i>),head(<i>x</i>)) if the first cell’s color is not <i>x</i>. Finally two neighboring palette colors may be swapped when their other fields agree and neither cell carries either kind of head. Because the other fields agree, this is also a swap of whole letters.')
    p('Declare zero a passed(<i>x</i>) cell of color <i>x</i>, any two adjacent palette cells of equal color, and a final palette head seeking <i>x</i> whose color differs from <i>x</i>. No other palette moves or palette zeros are used.')
    p('<b>Palette invariant.</b> Starting with each color once, no palette zero can be reached. Colors are preserved as a multiset. A checker cannot pass its sought color. Swaps cannot cross its head or the boundary between different checker states, so a passed prefix continues to exclude that color. A head therefore cannot finish incorrectly. Equal neighboring colors are impossible. Transaction moves, specified below, change neither colors nor checker fields.')
    p('Conversely, in a nonzero complete palette the checker can be removed. If a passed cell has the sought color there is already a zero; otherwise reverse its steps until its head reaches the first cell and reverse creation. Once the transaction track is idle too, all non-color fields agree. Repeated colors can then be swapped together to produce zero; a missing color can be sought all the way to the final failed check. Thus a nonzero idle palette has every color exactly once and can be sorted.')
    p('Acquisition begins by changing an idle <i>C</i>(1) to a cursor with <i>b</i> = 1 and chosen <i>l</i>, task and ε. Copy its idle data into slot ε and freely choose its other slot and certificate bit. A cursor may acquire its next cell only if that cell is transaction-idle and is the next internal position of its intended region. The old cursor becomes <i>L</i>, the next cell becomes the cursor, and its own structural position and relative block number determine the transported metadata. Existing core data are copied to slot ε; the other data and certificate bits are arbitrary. No payload is placed on a palette or stop.')
    p('The cursor cannot advance beyond its known last position. At that position alone it may turn into a full head in the initial verifier phase with all reading bits zero. All these moves have exact inverses. Inverse acquisition restores the selected slot to the idle data field; inverse creation removes the cursor. Acquisition and release are the only moves which can change stored payload.')

    page('16. A reversible verifier for a finite clause list')
    p('Temporarily fix a finite program string <i>a</i>. The next two pages construct from <i>a,h,l</i> and the task a nonempty list Φ<sub>1</sub>, …, Φ<sub>m</sub>, with <i>m</i> ≤ <i>q</i>. Each clause reads at most three bits: either a specified data slot at a specified relative block and core position, or a certificate bit in the first block. Pad shorter clauses by repeated addresses or an ignored dummy address. Both data slots are read for replacements; only slot zero is read for zero tasks. The clauses do not depend on ε.')
    p('For each clause <i>j</i> the full-head path is')
    eq('<i>A</i><sub>j</sub> ↔ reading tour ↔ before-gate<sub>j</sub><br/>↔ after-gate<sub>j</sub> ↔ undo tour ↔ <i>Z</i><sub>j</sub>.')
    p('Its endpoint phases occur only at the known last position. The head carries the clause index, one of these finitely many phases or the directional tour phases, ε, and three reading bits ρ. Every tuple ρ is syntactically permitted. A phase <i>A</i><sub>j</sub>, <i>Z</i><sub>j</sub>, or success with ρ ≠ 0 is a semantic unary zero. Such states remain syntactically present and can be endpoints of replacement edges. This distinction is part of the definition.')
    p('The reading tour starts at the last position, travels left, turns at the first, and returns right. A left step changes (<i>L,F</i>) to (<i>F,R</i>), and XORs into each reading register the addressed bit at the new head position, if any. A right step changes (<i>F,R</i>) to (<i>L,F</i>) and reads nothing. All payload and relative block numbers stay on their original cells; only head control, index, ε and reading registers move. The last position is a stop, never an address, so every addressed bit is read exactly once. Each turn, entry and exit is a unary phase change at its specified endpoint; each move has its exact inverse.')
    p('The gate from before-gate to after-gate exists exactly when Φ<sub>j</sub>(ρ) is true. A false after-gate state is zero. A false before-gate state has no gate edge but is not thereby zero. The undo tour travels left without reading, turns at first, and returns right, XORing addressed bits when leaving their cells. It exits at last into <i>Z</i><sub>j</sub>. Its phases are distinct from the reading-tour phases, and its inverse edges are included.')
    p('All tour entry and exit edges preserve every register tuple, even a tuple making an endpoint a semantic zero. Only the acquisition interface, the edge <i>Z</i><sub>j</sub> ↔ <i>A</i><sub>j+1</sub>, and the final edge <i>Z</i><sub>m</sub> ↔ success require zero registers. A replacement success with zero registers may flip ε; a zero-task success is a unary zero. Success has no shortcut back to acquisition. No phase is stored on the wings. This completely lists the verifier moves.')

    page('16. Correctness and rollback of the local protocol')
    p('Each listed move is instantiated on all unaffected fields allowed by the unary formats, with the indicated internal pair conditions for moves inside a region. In acquisition, test the changed pair as internal after the move. All moves and all zeros are unary or binary. There are no table entries of lengths three through <i>k</i>. Semantic zeros do not delete an otherwise listed replacement edge.')
    p('<b>Context and ownership.</b> Starting with complete canonical blocks, all syntactic pair rules survive every move. A new cursor at idle <i>C</i>(1) has an external predecessor and an idle successor inside the block. Acquisition changes the old cursor’s right interface from external to internal with its acquired neighbor; the new cursor has the external interface previously supplied by the idle cell. Its predecessor still permits an <i>L</i> or head with the same metadata. The known-last condition prevents an illegal last <i>L</i>. Release reverses these checks. A full-head move changes <i>F,R</i> to <i>L,F</i> or conversely, with exactly the same outside allowed interfaces. The endpoint change <i>B</i> ↔ <i>F</i> has identical external right interfaces. Checker moves preserve their stated grammar, and neither kind of move changes the other track.')
    p('Every acquired portion of a block contains its <i>C</i>(1), or continues a region which previously acquired that cell. A new transaction can start only at an idle <i>C</i>(1), and acquisition only takes idle cells. Thus two transactions cannot own cells in the same block. A region may be blocked by a later region at an external joint, but cannot acquire its cells until they are released. This proves the context and ownership assertions also when regions abut.')
    p('<b>Verification from acquisition.</b> While a region is full, all its payload is fixed, even if moves elsewhere are interleaved. Starting at <i>A</i><sub>j</sub> with ρ = 0, the reading tour puts exactly the addressed bits in the registers. The gate is crossed precisely when the clause is true, and the undo tour restores ρ = 0. An incorrect clause breaks the path before its after-gate state. Backtracking cannot bypass it because the only clause changes and acquisition interface are the listed ones. Thus success is reachable from acquisition exactly when every clause holds. No register or gate zero can be reached on such a path. A side flip changes no tested bit, so the path may then be fully reversed.')
    p('<b>Rollback from an arbitrary full state.</b> Every tour edge is a bijection on the register tuple for fixed payload. Reverse the prescribed path to its preceding endpoint. A reversed reading tour reaches <i>A</i><sub>j</sub>; nonzero registers there expose a zero. A reversed undo tour reaches after-gate; a false value exposes a zero, and a true one crosses the gate backwards and reverses the reading tour. A false before-gate state can also reverse its reading tour. At <i>A</i><sub>j</sub> with zero registers reverse the previous interclause edge and decrease the clause index. Success first reverses to <i>Z</i><sub>m</sub>. This reaches acquisition or a zero in finitely many reversals. At acquisition, inverse acquisition releases every acquired cell and inverse creation finishes. A cursor region can immediately be released. All these operations stay inside that region.')

    page('16. Explicit computation certificates')
    p('Fix a deterministic universal one-tape machine with fixed finite symbol and state alphabets. It interprets a supplied binary program, returns to a marked origin when accepting, and then freezes in a fixed accepting configuration. Such a machine can simulate each fixed program with polynomial overhead: encode its tape tracks and head marks and scan the used segment to implement each step; that segment grows at most linearly with elapsed simulated time. The return to the origin adds at most the segment length. Fix this machine and its finite transition function once and for all.')
    eq('<i>T</i> = 2<sup>⌊h/5⌋</sup>, &nbsp; <i>W</i> = 4<i>T</i>, &nbsp; <i>o</i> = 2<i>T</i>.')
    p('Use time rows 0 ≤ <i>t</i> &lt; <i>T</i>, tape cells 0 ≤ <i>j</i> &lt; <i>W</i>, and origin <i>o</i>. A tape cell, including a possible head state and the origin mark, has β bits, for an absolute constant β. The initial tape contains a self-delimiting encoding of the program <i>a</i>, parameter <i>Dh</i>, arity <i>l</i>, task, and the <i>lcDh</i> bits from slot zero, followed by those from slot one for replacements. For example encode an integer <i>n</i> as 1<sup>r</sup>0 bin(<i>n</i>), where <i>r</i> is its binary length and bin(0) = 0. Store this input immediately to the right of the origin; its size is O(<i>h</i>) for fixed <i>a,D</i>. For large <i>h</i> it occupies less than <i>T</i>/4 cells. A head cannot reach either boundary in <i>T</i> steps.')
    p('A machine step has a radius-one Boolean rule on the three adjacent cell encodings. Extend it to a total function on all bit patterns and fix a NAND circuit with <i>g</i> gates computing its β outputs. The constants β and <i>g</i> do not depend on <i>a,D,h</i>. Invalid patterns do not occur in a satisfying tableau because the initial row is valid and the next row is forced.')
    p('Allocate certificate bits <i>X</i>(<i>t,j,r</i>) for all cell bits and <i>Y</i>(<i>t,j,v</i>) for gate outputs, at these one-based core addresses:')
    eq('<i>X</i>(<i>t,j,r</i>) : 1 + β(<i>Wt</i> + <i>j</i>) + <i>r</i>,<br/>0 ≤ <i>r</i> &lt; β;')
    eq('<i>Y</i>(<i>t,j,v</i>) : 1 + β<i>WT</i> + <i>g</i>((<i>W</i>−2)<i>t</i>+<i>j</i>−1) + <i>v</i>,<br/>0 ≤ <i>t</i> &lt; <i>T</i>−1, &nbsp; 1 ≤ <i>j</i> &lt; <i>W</i>−1, &nbsp; 0 ≤ <i>v</i> &lt; <i>g</i>.')
    p('There are β<i>WT</i> + <i>g</i>(<i>W</i>−2)(<i>T</i>−1) certificate bits. They are stored in the first relative block only. The program string is part of the prescribed initial tape, not a control-head field.')

    page('16. Clauses and a finite rule-deciding program')
    p('List four clause families in this order, with their internal indices in lexicographic order. First, constrain every initial-row bit, using two clauses for equality to a data bit or its negation, or a repeated unit clause for a constant. Second, constrain both boundary cells at every later time to remain blank. Third, for every interior cell and nonfinal time constrain all NAND gates and then all next-row output bits. A gate <i>z</i> = ¬(<i>u</i> ∧ <i>v</i>) is expressed by')
    eq('(<i>u</i> ∨ <i>z</i>), &nbsp; (<i>v</i> ∨ <i>z</i>), &nbsp; (¬<i>u</i> ∨ ¬<i>v</i> ∨ ¬<i>z</i>).')
    p('Each output equality uses two clauses. Fourth, use β unit clauses fixing the final cell at the marked origin to its accepting head state and fixed origin symbol. Constants on circuit wires are permitted and can be ignored as inputs of the resulting Boolean clause. The total clause count is')
    eq('2β<i>W</i> + 2β(<i>T</i>−1)<br/>+ (3<i>g</i>+2β)(<i>W</i>−2)(<i>T</i>−1) + β.')
    p('Both this number and the certificate count are O(<i>T</i><sup>2</sup>) and thus at most <i>q</i> = 2<sup>h</sup> for all large <i>h</i>. Every clause has at most three addressed inputs. Induction on time shows that a satisfying assignment is precisely the actual computation and its circuit outputs. The boundary conditions do not interfere with that computation. The final constraint holds exactly when acceptance occurs before <i>T</i>, since earlier acceptance freezes. This proves both directions of the certificate test.')
    p('Given one clause index, comparisons with the four family sizes, followed by quotient and remainder, find its family, time, cell and bounded gate subindex. The displayed address formulas and the fixed circuit then produce its addresses and truth function. Initial-row lookup chooses a constant bit, a program bit, a parameter bit or an addressed data bit. All arithmetic is on O(<i>h</i>)-bit integers and takes polynomial time in <i>h</i> for fixed <i>a,D</i>. One never enumerates the whole tableau or the preceding clauses.')
    p('Define <i>P</i>(<i>a,h</i>; candidate) to decide whether a proposed unary or binary replacement or zero is in the table specified so far, using the clauses just defined with program string <i>a</i>. For replacements test both orientations. Check only the relevant formats, changed fields, interfaces, and at most one indexed clause. Include illegal formats as zeros, but do not suppress the listed moves with semantic-zero endpoints. All other candidates, including lengths three through <i>k</i>, are rejected. If any layout size, input length or field range does not fit, instead use the finite table declaring every letter zero and having no replacements. Its fit tests are explicit integer inequalities. The predicate <i>P</i> is polynomial-time for fixed <i>a,D</i>; it uses <i>a</i> as finite data and never executes it or searches for a certificate.')

    page('16. Closing the self-reference and choosing the parameter')
    p('There is an effective finite code constructor <i>s</i>(<i>t,v</i>) whose output, on input <i>x</i>, simulates the two-input program <i>t</i> on (<i>v,x</i>). For example its code consists of the stored strings <i>t,v</i> and a fixed interpreter wrapper. Simulation by scanning has polynomial overhead for each fixed stored program.')
    p('Take the two-input program <i>t</i> which forms the finite string <i>s</i>(<i>v,v</i>) from its first input <i>v</i> and computes <i>P</i>(<i>s</i>(<i>v,v</i>),<i>x</i>) on its remaining input. Set')
    eq('<i>a</i> = <i>s</i>(<i>t,t</i>).')
    p('Running this particular <i>a</i> on <i>x</i> returns exactly <i>P</i>(<i>a,x</i>). Constructing the fixed string <i>s</i>(<i>t,t</i>) costs a constant, while <i>P</i> and the interpreter overhead are polynomial in the parameter. This is a finite program definition. It is not a call by <i>P</i> to itself: <i>P</i> only checks local formats and the description of one Boolean clause.')
    p('The value <i>D</i> was fixed before constructing this code. Therefore the computation of <i>a</i> on each candidate at parameter <i>Dh</i>, including interpretation and return to the origin, has a polynomial time bound in <i>h</i>. Choose <i>h</i><sub>0</sub> so large that all format and size bounds hold, <i>q</i> ≥ 4, and that bound is less than 2<sup>⌊h/5⌋</sup> for every <i>h</i> ≥ <i>h</i><sub>0</sub>. Exponential growth supplies such an integer; a polynomial upper bound for the explicitly described routines also gives an effective choice. At these parameters an accepting certificate exists exactly for a genuine table entry at <i>Dh</i>.')
    p('All alphabet control fields fit uniformly. The structural position, color, sought color, block count, relative block number and clause index use fewer than 32 integer slots of width 2<i>h</i>; the largest indexed clause and certificate address is at most 2<sup>h</sup>. Tags, phase, selected side, slots and registers use fewer than 512 bits. Tape encodings and gates consume certificate positions and are not alphabet tags. The code length and the fixed value <i>D</i> are accommodated by taking <i>h</i><sub>0</sub> large; they do not enlarge <i>c</i> or <i>k</i>.')
    p('For each <i>h</i> = <i>h</i><sub>0</sub><i>D</i><sup>j</sup>, enumerate all unary and binary candidates on Σ<sub>h</sub> and keep those for which this fixed <i>P</i> returns one. The resulting alphabet and relation lists are finite. Every statement below concerns that finite table alone.')

    page('16. Preservation of nonzero encoded words')
    p('A genuine upper replacement on <i>l</i> letters can be simulated inside its <i>l</i> canonical blocks. Acquire them with their old data in slot zero, the proposed new data in slot one, and a satisfying certificate in the first core. The verified path reaches success, flips ε, reverses all verification, and releases the cells. This leaves the target encoding. A genuine upper zero is simulated in the same way, reaching its declared zero-task success. No exterior cell is used. Thus <i>E</i><sub>h</sub> carries every upper relation to a consequence of the lower relations, and defines an algebra homomorphism.')
    p('For nonzero preservation, start with the encoding of an arbitrary upper word and follow an arbitrary sequence of lower replacements. The context and palette invariants preserve structural and marker syntax, and each palette continues to contain every color once. At every moment read a represented upper word: an idle core contributes its idle data; an acquired core contributes the slot selected by its unique owning head. The ownership property makes this unambiguous. Acquisition and release preserve those data, even when unselected slots and certificates are freely changed after partial release. Checker and palette swaps do not change the represented word.')
    p('During every interval in which a head is full, its payload is fixed and its control path began at acquisition. The verification argument therefore applies even when other regions move. A replacement side flip occurs only with a certificate of a genuine upper replacement, so it changes the represented word by that replacement. A zero-task success would certify a genuine upper zero. Syntactic zeros, false-after-gate zeros and nonblank endpoint zeros cannot be reached from this valid start; palette zeros were excluded separately. These assertions are preserved at each step of the arbitrary path.')
    p('If the original upper word is nonzero, every represented upper word is equivalent to it and cannot contain an upper zero. Hence a zero-task success cannot occur either. No lower zero declaration is reachable. Lemma 16.1 proves that the encoded word is nonzero. Whole-word endpoints do not give any additional moves: an absent cell can only prevent a proposed acquisition.')
    p('There is a nonzero letter at each level. Choose a legal idle core letter <i>C</i>(<i>r</i>) with 1 &lt; <i>r</i> &lt; <i>q</i>, and legal data. It is not a unary zero, and the only unary transaction creation requires <i>C</i>(1). No binary move or zero applies to a one-letter word. It is therefore nonzero. Repeatedly encoding such letters gives nonzero words of unbounded lengths at a fixed level, using only finitely many encodings at any one time.')

    page('16. Arbitrary words and internal normalization')
    p('We must also control words with control states which never arose from clean blocks. In a nonzero word every local format and adjacency is legal, since their violations are declared zeros. Put a cut immediately before a <i>C</i>(1) letter if its transaction track is idle or its relative block number is 1. This condition uses only that single letter.')
    p('Any palette fragment has at most one transaction head, constant-metadata wings and possibly an idle suffix; no transaction can begin inside it. It also has at most one checker head and constant passed and idle runs. Removing the at most two head cells and refining these ordered partitions gives at most 16 intervals with identical non-color fields. This loose bound covers truncated fragments too. If a color repeated in one interval, its allowed swaps would bring equal colors adjacent and make the word zero. Each interval therefore has length at most <i>q</i>. A palette fragment has length at most 16<i>q</i> + 2, and a block or partial block at most 17<i>q</i> + 3.')
    p('Between core beginnings with no intervening cut, the relative block number must strictly increase, and cannot exceed <i>k</i>. An idle block or a new transaction would instead create a cut. Thus an interval without a cut has length at most')
    eq('<i>B</i><sub>h</sub> = (<i>k</i>+2)(17<i>q</i>+3),')
    p('including its two possible end fragments. The unary exclusions on a first <i>R</i> and a last <i>L</i> keep this bound valid at endpoints where a neighbor is absent.')
    p('An interval between consecutive cuts starts at <i>C</i>(1) and ends at <i>S</i>. If its first block is idle, no locked region can start inside it and a following block would introduce another cut. Otherwise its first cell has relative block number 1 and is <i>L</i>, <i>B</i> or <i>F</i>, never <i>R</i>. A chain of left wings must reach a head inside the interval: it cannot exit at an external interface, cross the next cut internally, or be <i>L</i> at its known last position. A full head forces its right wing to that last position. A cursor leaves the remainder of its current block idle; the next block would be another cut. Hence the interval contains one through <i>k</i> whole structural blocks and at most one transaction.')
    p('Remove each checker by its backward moves. Then roll back the transaction or release the cursor as already proved. If this exposed a zero, the original containing word would be zero by Lemma 16.1, a contradiction. Thus release succeeds. It uses only cells in the interval, as do checker removal and subsequent palette sorting. With both tracks idle, the palette test forces exactly one of each color. Sorting them gives precisely <i>E</i><sub>h</sub>(<i>u</i>) for the upper data word <i>u</i>, with 1 ≤ |<i>u</i>| ≤ <i>k</i>. No reachable-state assumption entered this rollback argument.')

    page('16. Completion of the hierarchy proof')
    p('There are finitely many literal cut intervals, since they contain at most <i>k</i> bounded blocks. Let the token set consist of the nonzero intervals of the form just analyzed, and for each choose one internally obtained upper normal form <i>u</i><sub>t</sub>. This finite set need not be decided by the rule program: it is used only in the proof and in finite sums. A canonical block has length <i>L</i><sub>h</sub>; length-preserving normalization gives')
    eq('[<i>t</i>] = [<i>E</i><sub>h</sub>(<i>u</i><sub>t</sub>)], &nbsp; |<i>t</i>| = <i>L</i><sub>h</sub>|<i>u</i><sub>t</sub>|, &nbsp; 1 ≤ |<i>u</i><sub>t</sub>| ≤ <i>k</i>.')
    p('A sufficiently long nonzero word has at least two cuts by the bound on intervals with no cut. Its prefix before its first cut and suffix beginning at its last cut are bounded by <i>B</i><sub>h</sub>; all intervening intervals are tokens. Because cuts are determined by single letters, this literal decomposition is unique. Restricting to a subword cannot create or shift a cut.')
    p('Consider a complete palette in any such token before normalization. Structural tags and lengths are unchanged by normalization, so this palette already has length <i>q</i>. At most two cells are heads, and at most 16 non-head intervals have homogeneous non-color fields. One of them has length at least (<i>q</i> − 2)/16, which is at least <i>q</i>/100 for the chosen parameters. Its adjacent transpositions are explicitly in the table. Every eligible run, not just this selected one, has distinct colors by the same repetition-to-zero argument. Eligibility and maximal endpoints test only the non-color fields and head flags.')
    p('This proves all three assertions of Theorem 16.2. Its word graph and every proof above are independent of the field. In particular, changing from characteristic two to any other characteristic does not change which word classes are zero or the token equalities. □')

    page('17. Cancellation over an arbitrary prime field')
    p('For a fixed prime <i>p</i>, work over any field <i>K</i> of characteristic <i>p</i>. Put <i>h</i><sub>j</sub> = <i>h</i><sub>0</sub><i>D</i><sup>j</sup>, <i>q</i><sub>j</sub> = 2<sup>h<sub>j</sub></sup>, <i>L</i><sub>j</sub> = 2<i>q</i><sub>j</sub> + 1 and <i>A</i><sub>j</sub> = <i>A</i><sub>h<sub>j</sub></sub>(<i>K</i>). Write <i>E</i><sub>j</sub> for the corresponding substitution. A letter test assigns <i>d</i> by <i>d</i> matrices <i>M</i>(<i>a</i>) over <i>K</i> to the letters at one level, extends multiplicatively to words, and forms')
    eq('<i>S</i><sub>j,n</sub> = ∑<sub>|w|=n</sub> <i>M</i>(<i>w</i>) ⊗ [<i>w</i>]<sub>j</sub>.')
    p('A chunk test is the finite labeled list and length sum of Section 7, now at level <i>j</i>. Distinct identifiers, including repeated word labels and different segmentations of the same word, index distinct summands. Its dimension is <i>d</i> and its label length bound is <i>b</i>.')
    p('<b>Lemma 17.1 (Direct cancellation).</b> <i>If q<sub>j</sub>/100 &gt; b((p−1)d<sup>2</sup>+3), every such chunk test vanishes at every sufficiently large word length.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> Discard the summands whose words are zero. In each remaining word erase only the color fields and choose the first maximal eligible run of length at least <i>q</i><sub>j</sub>/100. Existence follows from Theorem 16.2, and this choice depends only on the color-erased word. Select all complete chunks inside this run; at most two chunks cross its ends. If the run has length <i>r</i> and the selected packet has <i>m</i> chunks, <i>r</i> ≤ <i>mb</i> + 2<i>b</i>, so <i>m</i> &gt; (<i>p</i>−1)<i>d</i><sup>2</sup>.')
    p('Permute these complete chunks, preserving the exterior chunks. Their total interval does not change, and all its letters have the same non-color fields. Therefore the color-erased word, the selected run, and the exterior crossing chunks stay fixed. The selected packet in the new segmentation is exactly the packet being permuted. Allowed adjacent letter swaps show that all its words have the same nonzero algebra image. Thus no member is lost among the discarded summands.')
    p('The labels in a packet are distinct: each begins with a different color within the distinct-color run. Hence the identifiers are distinct and the permutation action is free, even when the original test allowed repeated labels. Applying the selection again to any member yields the same orbit. We have a genuine partition into full permutation orbits. Each orbit contributes')
    eq('<i>U</i>(∑<sub>σ∈S<sub>m</sub></sub> <i>X</i><sub>σ(1)</sub> ··· <i>X</i><sub>σ(m)</sub>)<i>V</i> ⊗ [<i>w</i>].')
    p('Lemma 6.1 makes this zero in characteristic <i>p</i>. Summing the finitely many orbits proves the assertion. □')

    page('17. Passing between chunks and letters')
    p('<b>Lemma 17.2 (Cut reduction).</b> <i>If all dimension-d chunk tests of label bound k eventually vanish at level j+1, all dimension-d letter tests eventually vanish at level j.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> Fix a letter test <i>M</i>. Use literal tokens <i>t</i> as chunk identifiers upstairs, with label <i>u</i><sub>t</sub> fixed by Theorem 16.2 and weight <i>M</i>(<i>t</i>). Let its length sums be <i>C</i><sub>m</sub>. Let <i>P</i> range over words of length at most <i>B</i><sub>j</sub> with no cut letter, including the empty word. Let <i>S</i> range over nonempty words of length at most <i>B</i><sub>j</sub> beginning with a cut letter and containing no other cut letter. For every sufficiently large <i>n</i>, uniqueness of the literal cut decomposition gives')
    eq('<i>S</i><sub>j,n</sub> = ∑ (<i>M</i>(<i>P</i>)⊗[<i>P</i>])<br/>· (id⊗<i>E</i><sub>j</sub>)(<i>C</i><sub>m</sub>) · (<i>M</i>(<i>S</i>)⊗[<i>S</i>]),<br/><i>m</i> = (<i>n</i>−|<i>P</i>|−|<i>S</i>|)/<i>L</i><sub>j</sub> ∈ ℕ<sub>0</sub>.')
    p('Every nonzero word occurs once; extra concatenations which are zero contribute zero. The homomorphism and the token equalities give the algebra factor with the displayed order of matrix weights. The upper length tends to infinity uniformly over the finite choices of ends. Eventual vanishing of <i>C</i><sub>m</sub> proves the claim. □')
    p('<b>Lemma 17.3 (Finite automaton).</b> <i>Over an alphabet of size s, a dimension-d chunk test with maximum label length b is a fixed d by d block of a letter test of dimension at most bs<sup>b</sup>d, at every word length.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> First sum weights with identical labels; distributivity preserves all chunk sums. Use states consisting of the empty word and all nonempty proper prefixes of retained labels. On reading a letter <i>a</i> from a state <i>v</i>, add an identity-weighted transition to <i>va</i> if this is a proper prefix, and a transition weighted by the label weight back to the empty state if <i>va</i> is a complete label. Both transitions may coexist. Sum weights for identical edges. The block matrix of these transitions is the letter weight.')
    p('Paths from the empty state back to itself correspond exactly to segmentations into labels: the return steps close the successive chunks and multiply their weights in the original order. Therefore its empty-to-empty block is the required chunk sum after summing words. There are at most ∑<sub>i=0</sub><sup>b−1</sup><i>s</i><sup>i</sup> ≤ <i>bs</i><sup>b</sup> states. The empty-label-list case uses zero letter matrices. Padding with zero blocks permits any larger target dimension for positive-length sums. □')

    page('17. Termination of the cancellation procedure')
    p('<b>Theorem 17.4.</b> <i>At level zero every fixed letter test, and every fixed finite chunk test, eventually vanishes over every field of characteristic p.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> Start with a letter-test dimension <i>d</i> at level zero. Define the dimensions of the successive upper chunk tests by')
    eq('<i>d</i><sub>j</sub> = <i>d</i> ∏<sub>i=1</sub><sup>j−1</sup> (<i>k</i>|Σ<sub>i</sub>|<sup>k</sup>) &nbsp; (<i>j</i> ≥ 1).')
    p('Cut reduction first gives dimension <i>d</i><sub>1</sub> = <i>d</i> upstairs. At each further step the automaton changes a bound-<i>k</i> chunk test at level <i>i</i> to a letter test of dimension at most <i>d</i><sub>i+1</sub> at that same level, and cut reduction moves it up one level as a chunk test. Since |Σ<sub>i</sub>| = 2<sup>ch<sub>i</sub></sup>,')
    eq('log<sub>2</sub>(<i>d</i><sub>j</sub><sup>2</sup>) ≤ 2 log<sub>2</sub><i>d</i> + 2(<i>j</i>−1)log<sub>2</sub><i>k</i><br/>+ (2<i>kc</i>/(<i>D</i>−1))(<i>h</i><sub>j</sub>−<i>h</i><sub>1</sub>).')
    p('The coefficient 2<i>kc</i>/(<i>D</i>−1) is less than one. Thus <i>h</i><sub>j</sub> − log<sub>2</sub>(<i>d</i><sub>j</sub><sup>2</sup>) tends to infinity. For the fixed prime <i>p</i>, at some finite level <i>J</i> one has')
    eq('<i>q</i><sub>J</sub>/100 &gt; <i>k</i>((<i>p</i>−1)<i>d</i><sub>J</sub><sup>2</sup>+3).')
    p('Lemma 17.1 now kills every test of the required dimension and bound at level <i>J</i>. Apply Lemmas 17.2 and 17.3 backwards through the finitely many levels. They give eventual vanishing at level zero. Each test has its own finite threshold: the finite end lists and their length dilations only change that threshold finitely many times. No common bound over all dimensions or matrices has been used.')
    p('Finally any fixed chunk test at level zero is a block of a letter test of some finite dimension by Lemma 17.3. Its eventual vanishing follows from the letter-test assertion. □')
    p('This argument is the only cancellation step in which the characteristic enters. The factor <i>p</i> − 1 is fixed during the passage up the hierarchy and is absorbed by the growing ratio <i>q</i><sub>j</sub>/<i>d</i><sub>j</sub><sup>2</sup>. The finite presentation at level zero itself is independent of <i>p</i>.')

    page('18. The constructed algebra for every prime')
    p('<b>Theorem 18.1.</b> <i>For every prime p, the finite level-zero rule table of Section 16 defines an infinite-dimensional finitely presented graded unital associative algebra R(p) = F<sub>p</sub> ⊕ I(p), with finite-dimensional homogeneous parts and matrix-nil positive ideal I(p).</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> Set <i>R</i>(<i>p</i>) = <i>A</i><sub>h<sub>0</sub></sub>(F<sub>p</sub>). Its alphabet and complete rule list are finite, and every relator has positive homogeneous degree. The empty word spans its degree-zero copy of F<sub>p</sub>. There are only finitely many words of each fixed length, so every homogeneous part is finite-dimensional.')
    p('Choose a nonzero letter at level <i>j</i>. Iterating <i>E</i><sub>0</sub>, …, <i>E</i><sub>j−1</sub> produces a nonzero word in <i>R</i>(<i>p</i>) of length <i>L</i><sub>0</sub> ··· <i>L</i><sub>j−1</sub>. These degrees strictly increase. By the grading their nonzero images are linearly independent, proving infinite dimension.')
    p('Let <i>T</i> be any finite matrix over <i>I</i>(<i>p</i>). Expand its entries as a finite sum of nonempty word images with numerical matrix coefficients. By Lemma 16.1, extending from F<sub>p</sub> to F<sub>p</sub>(<i>z</i>) preserves the same word basis and all its zero classes. Apply Theorem 17.4 to the chunks in that expansion with each weight multiplied by the central indeterminate <i>z</i>. Proposition 7.1 then extracts the coefficient corresponding to the number of matrix factors and gives <i>T</i><sup>a</sup> = 0 for all sufficiently large <i>a</i>. This holds for every finite matrix size and every such <i>T</i>, proving matrix nilpotence. □')
    p('The definition of <i>R</i>(<i>p</i>) uses only the finite level-zero presentation. Higher levels supply proofs of its properties; they are not an infinite family of defining relations. All roots and groups in the remaining sections use this particular algebra. Write <i>R</i> = <i>R</i>(<i>p</i>) and <i>I</i> = <i>I</i>(<i>p</i>) from now on. For <i>d</i> ≥ 1 the notation <i>R</i><sub>≥d</sub> also means <i>I</i><sub>≥d</sub>.')

    page('19. Compatible phases on long nonzero words')
    p('<b>Lemma 19.1.</b> <i>For Q = L<sub>0</sub> ··· L<sub>d</sub>, there is a threshold N such that each nonzero basis word of length at least N has phases σ(w), τ(w) in ℤ/Qℤ, constant on its word class, with τ(w) = σ(w) + |w|. If v is a subword of such a word w, begins at offset b and has length at least N, then σ(v) = σ(w) + b.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> First construct phases for literal nonzero words, going downwards from level <i>d</i> + 1, where the modulus is 1 and the length threshold is 1. Suppose level <i>j</i> + 1 has phases modulo <i>Q</i>′ = <i>L</i><sub>j+1</sub> ··· <i>L</i><sub>d</sub> with threshold <i>n</i><sub>j+1</sub> and the subword property. For the token decomposition <i>w</i> = <i>P</i><sub>w</sub><i>t</i><sub>1</sub> ··· <i>t</i><sub>r</sub><i>S</i><sub>w</sub>, use the previously fixed upper words and put <i>U</i>(<i>w</i>) = <i>u</i><sub>t₁</sub> ··· <i>u</i><sub>tᵣ</sub>.')
    p('Choose <i>n</i><sub>j</sub> above the token threshold and at least 2<i>B</i><sub>j</sub> + <i>L</i><sub>j</sub><i>n</i><sub>j+1</sub>. Then |<i>U</i>(<i>w</i>)| ≥ <i>n</i><sub>j+1</sub>. It is nonzero: otherwise the token equalities and the algebra homomorphism <i>E</i><sub>j</sub> would make <i>w</i> zero. Define')
    eq('σ<sub>j</sub>(<i>w</i>) = <i>L</i><sub>j</sub>σ<sub>j+1</sub>(<i>U</i>(<i>w</i>)) − |<i>P</i><sub>w</sub>|<br/>modulo <i>L</i><sub>j</sub><i>Q</i>′.')
    p('The factor <i>L</i><sub>j</sub> makes this independent of the chosen representative of the upper residue. If <i>v</i> is a long enough subword at offset <i>b</i>, its cuts are exactly the original cuts lying in it. Its complete tokens are a consecutive subsequence of those of <i>w</i>, so <i>U</i>(<i>v</i>) is a literal subword of <i>U</i>(<i>w</i>). If its upper offset is <i>a</i>, the lengths satisfy')
    eq('<i>b</i> + |<i>P</i><sub>v</sub>| = |<i>P</i><sub>w</sub>| + <i>L</i><sub>j</sub><i>a</i>.')
    p('The upper subword identity substituted into the definition now gives σ<sub>j</sub>(<i>v</i>) = σ<sub>j</sub>(<i>w</i>) + <i>b</i>. This completes the downward induction for literal words.')
    p('At level zero increase the threshold to <i>N</i> = 2<i>n</i><sub>0</sub> + <i>k</i>. A single elementary replacement touches at most <i>k</i> letters, so an unchanged prefix or suffix has length at least <i>n</i><sub>0</sub>. It has the same offset before and after the replacement. The literal subword property applied to this common piece makes the phases of the two words equal. Every word in a nonzero replacement class is nonzero, so chaining proves invariance on the entire class. Length invariance gives the same for τ. The subword identity survives the increased threshold. □')

    page('19. Prime-to-p phase moduli')
    p('For long nonzero words <i>u,v</i>, a nonzero product has matching middle phases and inherits the outside phases. Indeed, apply the subword identity to the prefix and suffix of their concatenation. Thus')
    eq('τ(<i>u</i>) ≠ σ(<i>v</i>) &nbsp; ⇒ &nbsp; <i>uv</i> = 0;')
    eq('<i>uv</i> ≠ 0 &nbsp; ⇒ &nbsp; σ(<i>uv</i>) = σ(<i>u</i>), &nbsp; τ(<i>uv</i>) = τ(<i>v</i>).')
    p('These implications also hold after reduction of the phases modulo any divisor of <i>Q</i>, by Lemma 8.3. In the current hierarchy <i>L</i><sub>j</sub> = 2<sup>h<sub>j</sub>+1</sup> + 1. Apply Lemma 8.1 with <i>m</i> = <i>h</i><sub>j</sub> + 1, and for every <i>d</i> put')
    eq('<i>Q</i><sub>d</sub> = ∏<sub>j=0</sub><sup>d</sup> <i>L</i><sub>j</sub>, &nbsp; ℓ<sub>d</sub> = <i>Q</i><sub>d</sub>/<i>p</i><sup>v<sub>p</sub>(Q<sub>d</sub>)</sup>.')
    p('Corollary 8.2 proves that ℓ<sub>d</sub> is coprime to <i>p</i> and tends to infinity. Reducing the phases supplied by Lemma 19.1 modulo ℓ<sub>d</sub> gives compatible start and end phases modulo these arbitrarily large prime-to-<i>p</i> integers. The length threshold may depend on the chosen modulus. No common threshold over all moduli is asserted.')
    p('The following group argument uses one such modulus ℓ and its corresponding threshold <i>N</i>. It will turn the phase identities into an actual identity <i>z</i><sup>ℓ</sup> = 1 in the stable Steinberg group. This is the additional group assertion needed by Proposition 9.1.')
